Z||000000||Richard Parnell||03-22-2000||01:49 PM||||I guess I don't understand the DIS ignition at all on my car (can hardly wait to get the 350 in it).  Since I bought my 87 Coupe 3-4 years ago, when I change the plugs, I change the plug wires.  I usually just replace the wires one at a time so everything will be correctly connected, I thought.  The other day I was checking out the DIS coil to plug diagram and found that two of the plug wires were going to plugs that were out of sequence.  So I took a chance and hooked every thing up like the book and diagram in the engine compartment shows.  To my surprise, the engine starts and runs exactly the same as with the two wires switched.???? What gives? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Ozzy||03-22-2000||02:11 PM||||Richard...<P>I have DIS on my coupe. The same thing was on my Duke engine when I recently placed new wires.<P>The DIS system produces a waste spark in the opposing cylinder. (I.E. Cyl. 1 is on compression while cyl. 3 is on exaust stroke and a spark is produced in each.) If 1 & 3 or 2 & 4 are transposed the engine will still run because there will be a spark in any case.<P>I swapped mine thinking I had solved 'ALL' my performance problems, only to find that there was no change att all.<P>(Did I doo good Ogre???)<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000002||Richard Parnell||03-22-2000||02:58 PM||||Thanks OZZY- It's nice to know I'm not the only one that got all excited thinking my car would really move after switching the wires to the "correct" sequence.  What a strange car car-- but I love the way it drives and looks.||||reg||7
Z||000003||theogre||03-22-2000||07:18 PM||||yeah, DIS only cares that the cylinders are paired, and the pair on the correct coil. Physics takes care of firing.||||reg||1
Z||000004||BroCar 87||03-23-2000||11:51 PM||||i have DIS also on my 87 coupe... What two go on what coil??? or does it not even matter, I understand that A certain two have to be together... Which ones and would they be on the top coil or bottom coil???||||reg||5
Z||000005||theogre||03-24-2000||09:00 AM||||follow the normal firing order. that pairs the cyls right. I forget at the moment if you count from the top or the bottom of the coils. I'll have to look later. Amazingly this actually is in the haynes book..... ||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||03-24-2000||07:58 PM||||If you switch the wires the only thing that will happen is the polarity of the current flow will be reversed. That is the spark will come from the outer electrode to the center electrode instead of the other way around. This will cause the outer electrode to wear instead of the center electrode. Both coils fire at the same time in a DIS system, one on the exhaust stroke and one on the power stroke, which propels the cylinder.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||03-25-2000||02:01 PM||||88formula,,,, <P>The coils are internally Y'd to the towers. The coils do not care at all which wire of a cylinder pair is on which terminal. It only matters that the right plug pair is on the right coil. <P>I don't know how you got the idea polarity will be reversed. It's not posible.<P>Others,<BR>Acording to Haynes the coil controller does still have full control thru 600RPM similar to the distributor motors.||||reg||1
